I have a couple of stories that made me laugh in the past week, but which I'm not going to post.  No one reads these pages, but there are people who I really like who might be pained to see these stories on the internet.

The bad part of that is that I can think of nothing else to write.  So, to open the dam, I'm going to put some things to page that never see the light of day.  Or maybe some day I un-hide this post.  Who knows?  

On Wednesday last week, we went to Stacie's Dad's funeral.  Rest in peace, Mr. Steve.  Stace's parents were always so good to us.

It was the full Catholic Church treatment.  To my heathen eyes, it was kind of weird, with the waving around of incense and the coffin and the shroud and all, but I do sort of appreciate the ritual of it all.

The church was layed out where there is a raised platform sort of in the middle, but you walk down even to go behind the platform.  After comunion, the little alter boy tripped on his robe and face planted down the stairs.

I had to stop myself from running over and picking him up.  It's a strager's kid in a room full of strangers.  Surely he has family there.  

But then no one else picked him up either.  He went to his chair behind the main proceedings and cried quitely rubbing his head while an old lady sat next to him making barely comforting gestures.

I really wanted to text Stacie Sunday and ask if the alter boy had cleared the concussion protocol, but you know, the whole keeping my jokes from being painful to others....  Your writer here is becoming an adult.

And speaking of adults, a bunch of them were over for college games Saturday night.  It gave me the chance to overhear this:

Maria:  So you have three children, is that right?

Amanda:  Yes.

Maria:  And none of them is retarded?

And she wasn't being mean att all.  There's not a mean thought in Maria's head ever.  She asked it just as if she were asking, "and do they all have blond hair?"

Oh goodness, I've made myself laugh out loud thinking of that exchange for two days straight.

Maybe I'm not becoming an adult.